t is a clear tightening of conditions, but not a shut market.\n- **Issuance:** Norvane priced a C$1.1B bond \"comfortably\" in Q3. That shows investors will absorb utility paper at the wider spreads.\n- **Equity, for contrast:** Equity is the weak channel. Pellham's C$2.4B share offering was pulled on 14 October, and a second utility offering was cut back (pellham-offering). Pellham says it will cut 2027 transformer orders by about 40% and resize its Q1 2027 tender. So debt is the funding route that works, and the equity gap is feeding through to 2027 capex.\n- **Rates and capacity:** I found nothing on the policy rate path or on utility leverage and debt headroom. The search returned no documents on either. I can't say whether the utilities have the balance-sheet room to borrow more. Pellham's choice to cut orders instead of leaning harder on debt hints that it may not, but that is only an inference.\n\n**Verdict:** Norvane can likely fund its 2027 plan through debt at higher cost. Pellham is the weak point. It is the largest buyer of grid equipment in Caldria, and its 2027 spending is already being scaled back. Debt alone doesn't appear to be filling the gap left by the failed equity raise. Treat the capacity conclusion as unverified until leverage and rate data turn up.","ttft_ms":1617,"type":"result","duration_ms":33887,"uuid":"4ff6da10-5325-4ace-a8f6-f56d1478ebe3","ttft_stream_ms":920,"time_to_request_ms":63,"time_to_request_phases_ms":{"other":5,"system_prompt":5,"resume_other":0,"process_user_input":3,"input_attachments":13,"input_hooks":2,"conversation_append":1,"transcript_persist":2,"tool_pool_refresh":0,"init_message":5,"init_frame_send":2,"engine_pickup":1,"loop_entry":3,"message_prep":1,"model_config_wait":1,"autocompact":1,"request_announcements":6,"query_setup":0,"model_gates":1,"tool_schema_build":2,"message_normalization":2,"client_creation":2,"request_build":5},"turn_start_resume_kind":"none","process_turn_index":0,"time_to_request_loop_lag_ms":13,